WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.300
Measure L asks the voters of this city whether an elected mayor should serve a two-year term.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:16.800
The City Council put three mayor questions on this ballot: K on electing the mayor, and L and M on a two-year or a four-year term.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.530
Four of the five council members voted to place them, in June 2025.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:28.040
Today, the council chooses one of its own members to preside as mayor each year.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:35.560
The two-year ordinance would give an elected mayor a two-year term, and each of four council members a four-year term.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:42.360
That ordinance is written to follow a majority vote both for an elected mayor and for a two-year term.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.450
The first mayoral election would be in November 2028.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.190
Each of the three measures needs a majority vote to pass.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:55.780
The county elections page for this election does not include a cost estimate for the measure.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:01.360
No argument for Measure L appears among the official documents gathered for this video.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:07.330
No argument against Measure L appears among the official documents gathered for this video.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.510
A yes vote on Measure L answers yes to a two-year term for the mayor.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.990
A no vote on Measure L answers no to a two-year term for the mayor.
